      A: { title: "Birds That Fly Far", text: "Every year, many birds take long trips. This is called migration. In the cold months, there is little food in the north. So birds fly south to warmer places. When spring comes, they fly back home. The Arctic tern flies the farthest of all. It flies from the top of the Earth to the bottom and back every year. That is about 70,000 kilometers! How do birds find the way? They use the sun and the stars. Some birds come back to the same tree every year." },
