    part: 249, slug: "unseen-reading-part-249", theme: "Electricity and Light", blurb: "where electricity comes from, how a light bulb works",
    texts: {
      A: { title: "Where Electricity Comes From", text: "Electricity makes our lights, fans and phones work. But where does it come from? Most electricity is made in power plants. Inside a power plant, there is a big machine called a generator. It has magnets and coils of wire. When the magnets spin, they push electricity through the wire. To spin the generator, power plants use moving water, wind or steam. Some steam comes from burning coal or gas. Solar panels are different. They turn sunlight straight into electricity. Then wires carry the electricity to our homes." },
      B: { title: "How a Light Bulb Works", text: "Long ago, people used candles and oil lamps at night. In 1879, Thomas Edison made a light bulb that could shine for many hours. Inside his bulb was a thin wire. Electricity made the wire so hot that it glowed. But most of the energy became heat, not light. Today, many homes use LED bulbs. An LED makes light in a different way. Its tiny parts glow without getting very hot. LED bulbs use much less electricity. They can also last for many years." },
    },
