    texts: {
      A: { title: "How Caves Are Made", text: "Many caves are made by water, very slowly. Rain falls and goes into the ground. On the way, it becomes a little sour, like a weak acid. Some rocks, like limestone, slowly dissolve in this water. It is a bit like sugar in water, but much slower. Over thousands of years, the water makes small holes in the rock. The holes get bigger and bigger. Finally, they become caves. Some caves are giant. Son Doong in Vietnam is one of the biggest caves in the world. A whole forest grows inside it!" },
      B: { title: "Icicles Made of Stone", text: "Inside some caves, there are pointed rocks hanging from the ceiling. They look like icicles. They are called stalactites. Water drips from the cave ceiling. Each drop leaves a tiny bit of rock behind. Over many years, the bits build a long stone point. Where the drops fall on the floor, other rocks grow up. These are called stalagmites. Here is a trick to remember: stalactites hold tight to the ceiling. They grow very slowly, often about one centimeter in a hundred years. So never touch or break them." },
    },
