      A: { title: "Snowflakes", text: "Snow forms high in cold clouds. A tiny drop of water freezes around a bit of dust. More water freezes onto it, and it grows into a snowflake. Almost all snowflakes have six sides or six arms. That is because of the way water freezes. As a snowflake falls, it passes through air of different temperatures. This changes how its arms grow. Every snowflake takes a different path. So scientists say no two snowflakes are exactly alike. The biggest snowflakes can be about the size of a coin." },
      B: { title: "Why Ice Floats", text: "Put an ice cube in a glass of water. It floats! Most things get heavier when they become solid. Water is different. When water freezes, it takes up a little more space. So ice is lighter than the same amount of water. This is very important for fish. In winter, lakes freeze on the top first. The ice floats and covers the lake like a blanket. Under the ice, the water stays cold but liquid. Fish can still swim and live there until spring. If ice sank, many lakes would freeze from the bottom up." },
