    part: 262, slug: "unseen-reading-part-262", theme: "Your Amazing Body", blurb: "your hard-working heart, why we yawn",
    texts: {
      A: { title: "Your Hard-Working Heart", text: "Your heart is about the size of your fist. It is a strong muscle that pumps blood around your body. Blood carries oxygen and food to every part of you. Your heart beats about 100,000 times every day. It never takes a break, even when you sleep! When you run or play, your heart beats faster. Your body needs more oxygen, so the heart works harder. You can feel your heartbeat. Put two fingers on the side of your neck. Count the beats for one minute. Exercise keeps your heart strong." },
      B: { title: "Why We Yawn", text: "Everybody yawns, even babies before they are born. Cats, dogs and fish yawn too. But why do we do it? Scientists are still not sure. One idea is that yawning cools the brain. When we yawn, we take in a big breath of air. This may help the brain stay cool and awake. We often yawn when we are tired or bored. Yawns are also catching. If you see someone yawn, you may yawn too. Even reading about yawning can make you yawn! Are you yawning now?" },
    },
