    part: 268, slug: "unseen-reading-part-268", theme: "Milk and Cheese", blurb: "milk for strong bones, holes in the cheese",
    texts: {
      A: { title: "Milk for Strong Bones", text: "Milk comes from cows, goats and other animals. Many people drink it every day. Milk has calcium. Calcium helps build strong bones and teeth. Your bones keep growing until you are about 20 years old. So kids need a lot of calcium. Milk also has protein, which helps your muscles grow. You can get calcium from other foods too. Cheese, yogurt, small fish with bones and green vegetables have it. Some people get a stomachache from milk. They can drink special milk or eat other calcium foods." },
      B: { title: "Holes in the Cheese", text: "Some cheese, like Swiss cheese, is full of round holes. Where do the holes come from? Cheese is made from milk. Cheese makers add tiny living things called bacteria. In Swiss cheese, some bacteria make a gas. The gas makes bubbles inside the cheese while it slowly gets older and harder. When the cheese is cut, the bubbles show as holes. Cheese makers call them eyes. Scientists also found that tiny bits of hay in the milk help the holes form. Very clean modern milk has fewer bits, so some cheeses now have fewer holes." },
    },
